The Kagi Charts are supposed to appear in 70s of the 20th century, at the first stage of Japanese stock market development.
If price move on in the same direction, the vertical line in the chart becomes longer.
The thickness of Kagi lines changes when prices exceed their previous maximum/minimum values.
A sequence of thin lines means that the supply is above demand (the market drops).
The basic trade signal on a Kagi chart is the line thickness: If a thin line becomes thick, you should buy, if vice versa, you should sell.
