As a refugee, you may hear many rumors about America that are not true before you are resettled.
I will mention some of these misconceptions and rumors and only focus on some of them.
The sources for these misconceptions and generalizations depend on several factors.
In this short story, I will try to recall some of these misconceptions and rumors that we heard as refugees before entering to the United States of America in the 2012.
Many refugees and immigrants were always talking about gun violence in American society.
When I asked them why, they usually talked about violence and killing in streets, kidnapping kids for money and so on.
Even the day before traveling to airport she told me that someone in America will kidnap your son.
Others told me that you are going to be regretted if you stay in the United States of America.
Almost all of them never been to the United States of America.
My mom, for example, is illiterate and never watched or listened to anything about America.
These movies can affect a lot on how people think about an issue.
Along with other factors, Hollywood movies might not transfer the exact picture of the United States of America to the World.
Most refugees, before entering the United States of America believe that Americans are not friendly, do not like strangers, or immigrants.
Still, I do not know why they think like this or where this rumor come from.
Or, it may be because Americans are more private than some communities but this does not mean they are unwelcoming.
When we entered to Unites state of America, I did not see guns in streets are I did not see any kind of street violence.
I also learned that all of the fears about African Americans are wrong and simply people being fearful and racist.
During our four years in Illinois, we had received a lot of support from American families, government and even churches supported us a lot.
Now, our new community is very refugee-friendly.
All these experiences would tell me one thing; rumors and generalizations are not true about America.
We found ourselves in a very nice and calm community.
(And I will tell my mom that no one kidnapped my son in America.)
