Thankful Beyond Words: 9 things I am thankful for in life
As an English Language Learner who is relatively new to the United States, Google has become a close friend.
Life was fine without it, but now I am thankful to have access to the internet.
According to Google, being thankful is showing your gratitude for something or someone.
What does it mean for you to be thankful?
A Global Perspective: My unique experiences have opened my eyes to things that others might easily take for granted.
Mikey: The man who was there for me when I needed help.
Landlord: A Russian immigrant who gave me a chance after I had given up on finding an apartment due to having no credit or work history here.
Employment Specialist: She helped me to find my first entry-level job after my arrival and assured me that life would only get better because dreams can come true in America.
A group that taught me about the true meaning of serving others without asking for anything in return.
My Clients: They teach me to genuinely appreciate life, to humble myself despite disagreements in order to find solutions, and to attempt new problem-solving strategies.
A city that gave me hope and allowed me to thrive.
Advisor: She patiently walked me through the steps of enrolling in a master’s program and put me on a professional track towards success.
She left her career to raise our kids and she welcomes me home with a smile and hug after a long day as she reminds me that I work too much (I have yet to be convinced, but I know it’s true).
In Iraq, I sheltered my feelings to keep my sanity and did not allow myself to dream to avoid disappointment.
My advice for others is to work hard, be faithfully dedicated and to surround yourself with positive people.
