you can see the link below.
According to the dictionary, a feeling is “an emotional state or reaction,” and we all have good and not so good feelings.
The other day, I was reading to my two-year-old son about feelings and the different expressions we as humans can have.
We got to the page that talks about ‘fear;’ the character in the story feared darkness and thunder.
I was excited to see his engagement with the book.
You guessed it right… now every night we have to talk about why we shouldn’t fear darkness and trust that God is always there to protect us no matter what.
However, my own experience was different.
I grew up in a culture that discourages freedom of expression when it comes to emotions; this is perhaps so you can navigate the harsh reality without losing your mind, or be becoming a victim.
Many refugees have the same struggle of not being able to articulate what they don’t know much about – specifically feelings.
It took me three years to come to the realization that I have issues – not them; how normal people have something called FEELINGS!!
I remember this like it was yesterday when a client of mine told me his story and I started trembling, not because of what he went through, but rather because I began feeling sympathetic inside and I was insecure in feeling that way.
That living in isolation from my surroundings is the better option.
Take a deep breath and carefully take time to recognize what you are feeling.
You can communicate your feelings by your physical movement, your mannerism and the way you respond to others.
You might face dark clouds on your journey.
Yet it is who you are today, the future is in your hands and you ought to live it to the fullest.
