Be open to your community and share your culture
That orientation was actually important for me in setting my goals of life in the U.S. then and later as a refugee.
I participated in various social activities on and off campus and gave cultural talks telling people about my home country and culture.
Upon doing all that, I found people of the host community very interested in accepting me, providing all sorts of assistance to me and getting to know me better.
So when my scholarship was over two years later, I was in tears having to go back home and leaving behind all the great friends I had met and the wonderful community that had embraced me with open arms.
Being forced out from our own homes
My family and I had to live a harsh life as internally displaced people for years to come.
Coming to America and finding a welcoming community
Again, my family and I found a welcoming community in the same state, and the amount of kindness and generosity we found here has made us feel like home.
I guess the key for refugees to thrive in the new community in the U.S. is to be open-minded.
