Like many things in life, good leadership can be learned through trial and error.
One sure way to become a great leader is to watch and listen to people who have already been proven their leadership abilities.
The idea is that people who have come to recognize their own strengths, fears, and desires are better suited to use their own for the benefit as well as that of those around them.
A good balance of EIQ, IQ, and integrity can get anyone far in life.
They even practice it in their daily lives in order to make the best of it.
I have met very few people who have mastered the art of genuinely and effortlessly empowering others through positivity.
I doubt they pick up the phone with the intention to make me feel this way.
In their own positivity, they are able to create solutions for nearly every issue that arises.
Leaders recognize that the best ideas are not always their own
I had realized that the way things had always been done took much more time than necessary to achieve fewer results.
My work produced the results I needed, but my boss was not pleased.
The best people I know are open to hearing what others think.
Good leaders take the time to figure out why a project is not producing the desired results before either continuing to work on it or set it aside.
When an idea is important enough, failure is not an option.
In leadership, one has to truly believe this and encourage everyone else to do the same.
