An outlook on the English language from a bilingual learner
Photo of Wilson Kubwayo after arriving in America in 2008.
In 2008, my family migrated from a Tanzania refugee camp to America.
However, I thought my dad spoke English because as a man of the family, he had to speak for all of us.
It was early February and I remember seeing clear at night as we drove through the city.
I was used to dark nights where you are not able to recognize anything in front of you.
The concept of having light gave me hope.
I literally did not speak any English!
In my thick accent, I said “Fanks” as I reflected how fast Americans spoke English.
Today I still ask myself, “Do I speak English now?”
I remember my first day of school in America.
Luckily, they called a student name Egide who spoke my native tongue, Kirundi.
My middle school was a big campus.
Personally, I wanted to look like the other students I have seen in music videos.
Words cannot express the smile I had on my face.
I just stood there and watched the rest of my team follow a recipe to make food.
I was asked to hold a tablespoon as another student poured salt in it to measure how much salt we were going to use.
I had a lot of questions in mind but I did not know how to ask them in English.
Finally, a student asked me something in English.
At least I thought.
I always said the same thing to anyone who tried to converse with me; “No English.” Now that I think of it, it is somewhat embarrassing.
I felt good to speak some English!
Why I learned English at such fast pace
Most of them were students making fun of me because of my inability to speak the English language.
It was after school when two students approached me.
One of them pushed me as they said something stupid like, “you must be in sixth grade.” They tried to say something to find a reason to fight me.
At this point, I wished to have spoken the English language.
I wished to have friends nearby who could speak for me.
It was not because they pushed me around, but because I could not even talk.
It just did not feel right.
However, I was determined to fight back.
I had faced more difficulties in my past than two middle schoolers who did not have better things to do.
I ignored everything and ran to catch my bus.
From moments where I wanted to speak out for myself when I faced challenges or moments where I wanted to join a friendly conversation, I longed the power to speak English.
In addition, I am a social and outgoing person.
How bad do you want to learn English?
It is always about how bad you want that which you long for in life.
I did not know how to read English, but I read anyway.
But due to how bad I wanted to speak English after arriving in America, I read an English dictionary and tried to learn as many words as possible.
I watched a lot of funny movies and repeated certain words that were easier to say.
Attending school was something extra when it comes to learning the English language for me.
I hated school very much.
The experience is different for everyone.
Sometimes I translated words in my language and tried to say them in English and my teachers would not understand me at all.
In addition, classes were very difficult for me.
They got angry with us sometime, but I applause their patients and their skills to teach different students from different countries with different backgrounds.
It is through difficulties and challenges that I learned the English language so fast.
I started a conversation when it means I had to do the uncomfortable.
I did whatever it took to improve my ability to speak and understand English.
There are certain words that I know in my language that I could not tell you what they are in English.
English speakers still use words such as “like” “you know what I mean” to get their point across.
I have been in America for nine years and people say my English is great.
People say I speak better English with only nine years of experience of it.
