This page on American culture for Somalis has information to help you understand some of the main differences you may experience as a Somali immigrant to the US.
Like Somalia, the United States was once under colonial rule.
Like Somalia today, it took many years for the US to rebuild after the war.
Americans place high value on the idea of free speech.
This is different than Somalia culture where you typically show respect for elders.
History While the civil war in Somali was relatively recent, the US civil war took place in the 1800s (over 150 years ago).
However, like in Somalia, there are still some negative feelings about the civil war.
Education In the US, children are required to attend school from ages 5-18.
Going to community college, a trade school, or university is a good way to get a better paying job.
You may notice your child develops this trait in the US.
In US schools, parents are expected to participate in their children’s education.
Like in Somalia, Agriculture is an important business in the US.
All businesses are regulated by the government and required to pay taxes to the government.
Some choose to make donations to causes they personally support.
Religion While the US is a very diverse country with people from many different faiths, many people follow Christian values and celebrate Christian holidays.
The US constitution says that the government must not follow or give priority to any particular religion.
Like Islam, Christianity follows one God.
If you a practicing Muslim, you can talk to your employer about your faith and ask for a place to pray during the day.
People typically celebrate holidays by getting together with their families and by eating large meals together.
Birthdays are also celebrated in the US, often with cake and friends.
Unlike in Somali, people do not typically acknowledge the day someone dies, although some people may feel sad on that day or visit the person’s grave if they were buried at a cemetery.
Some Somalis say they do not have any skills for the American workforce.
Doctors are very respected in the US.
If a doctor will not talk to you about your family member’s medical care, do not take this as a sign they are being rude but remember it is because of America’s laws.
In the US, most women choose to take their husbands last name when they get married, although some choose to keep their own last name.
Sometimes, if you have a different last name than your child, you may be required to show proof that the child is your child. Elders
This may be a hard adjustment for Somali elders.
Unlike in Somalia where people will wait to leave an activity until it is finished, in the US, people will leave events early to make sure they are on time to their next appointment.
However, it is for a woman to decline to shake hands or to touch a man.
However, it is ok to touch other men.
Family members often touch and hug each other. Communication
If you write something down or sign a piece of paper in the US, people will take the paper very seriously.
People in the US may not seem friendly compared to Somalis.
In the US, people may say hi in the morning or smile but do not take time to greet each other.
Saying Thanks
This may be different than in Somalia, where it is not necessary to say thanks.
So this is one thing you can do to help adjust to American culture.
In the US, people mostly eat with silverware (fork, knife and spoon).
If you have a guest at your home, you can offer them their food on a separate plate.
People in the US eat a lot of wheat or carbs.
Meat is often expensive in the US, especially halal meat.
This means many Somalis see money as something to be shared and will give money to any family or friend who is in need.
It is very common for Americans to see a counselor to help them through the death of a family member or other sad and hard events.
If your student needs to see a counselor, this can be a big help for your family and does not mean anything negative for your family.
Unlike in Somalia, only a small percentage of families live with their extended families.
Men help with household chores such as cooking and cleaning and women may work outside of the house.
Most families in the US have two children, and that rate is getting lower.
were designed for families with four or less members as compared to the large families most Somalis have.
Somalis tend to have a strong community and family network where many women can help take care of the children.
In the US, many people pay to send their children to daycare.
In the US, men and women live, work, and go to school together.
You will probably have to attend more co-ed events in the US than in Somalia.
Girls in the US are expected to continue with their educations just like boys.
American girls are not expected to run their households until their are older or getting married.
In the US, women/girls are still expected to go to work and school while they are menstruating.
The US provides very little support for new moms.
In Somalia, most women spend 40 days resting after childbirth.
Men typically return to work very quickly after the birth of their child.
The US government does require employers to give un-paid leave depending on where you work.
You can visit our forums to ask other immigrants for advice or to share your story.
