So I was admitted as a freshman in high school.
It was hard for me to begin with, because I didn’t speak much English.
I didn’t have anyone from my community at school to start off with, in my freshman year.
However, to overcome all of these problems, I just focused on my study.
The one thing I always put it in my mind is, no matter how strange this country feel for me, this is what I came for: I came here for a better life so I have to try my best.
I asked a lot of questions in class during the lecture time.
It’s made a lot different when you have a one-on-one time with your teacher.
I found out that, staying after school is so beneficial for me.
Every teacher are willing to help me and they actually love it when I asked them questions.
That the reason I did my homework without pressure and I got A’s or B’s in all of my classes in all these years of schooling.
I went to the library, searched for a good, knowledgeable books and started reading.
As I read and when I come across to the words that I don’t understand, I stop, take a look of dictionary, and learned more vocabulary words as I go through the whole book.
Finally, I also like to study as a group.
When we got together, we asked questions, exchanged ideas, and took turns doing flash cards to get ready for the test.
Also it’s is more fresh mind to study with a friend than to be by yourself.
You might have language issue or some other problems, but there is always a way to solve the problem.
You are in this country for a better life and do it as the way it is.
