In her social media posts, Maria comes across as a hardworking, pleasant young woman.
She and her husband own a six-acre farm on which they farm vegetables and fruits of all kinds.
When she lists their most recent harvest, I find myself asking her to describe many of the fruits she mentioned.
She recalls her journey to becoming a successful farmer with pride.
I could hear the familiar sounds of children seeking their Mama’s attention.
She is most pleased that she has a chance to raise her children the way she wants, instead of sending them to daycare while working for someone else.
In fact, she points out that the reason she became involved in farming was her a desire to raise a family with the freedom to care for them on your own terms.
Less than a decade ago, Maria was a child living with her nine siblings and her parents in a refugee camp in Tanzania.
Not long after, Maria met her now husband, who had a vision of farming in Hawaii.
For the past eight years, Maria and her husband have worked tirelessly to grow their business.
It will be yet another way in which she can share the harvest from her farm with others.
She is a firm believer in never giving up even when the road to your goals is filled with hurdles.
In Maria’s case, she set out to do farming, feed her family, her community, and build a future.
She is most proud that she is giving her children a great foundation for the future that is much brighter than she could have imaged.
The message from the Hebrew Immigrant Aid Society (HIAS) is a reminder of the Jewish immigrants who arrived in the United States, fleeing wars and seeking freedom, opportunity, and a better life.
They met on a train heading for a German refugee camp after surviving the Nazi Holocaust.
My own family’s immigrant and refugee history comes to mind when I think of my colleague in the Minnesota House of Representatives, Rep. Ilhan Omar.
Ilhan was eight years old when the civil war erupted in Somalia.
Ilhan arrived in the United States in her teen years and first experienced discrimination as the only Muslim student and person of color in her Virginia junior high school.
Ilhan developed a keen interest in how politics can improve people’s lives.
In a short period of time, Ilhan Omar has made a significant contribution to Minnesota politics and local and national movements for social justice.
When I speak at schools in the Minneapolis legislative district I represent, the first question young Somali students ask me is, “Do you know Ilhan Omar?” When I reply, “Yes!” their faces brighten, and I know that Ilhan’s accomplishments are a source of pride and power for them.
As the son of refugees, I am inspired by her story and experiences.
Her example is changing the political culture of our community and paving the way for others to follow her lead.
