It was a really exciting session because it meant that we had made it past the most challenging parts of being vetted for resettlement.
My biggest take away from that session was explained by the trainer in no uncertain terms.
He explained that if you take away the different customs, cultures, and languages, the country could be unrecognizable.
He wanted us to never fear to wear our traditional clothes, to still cook the dishes we grew up eating, and mostly, to never forget our language.
That being aware of our origins needed no reminding.
For one, refugees and immigrants are encouraged to assimilate in order to succeed.
As a result, many families find themselves leaving their old selves completely behind in order to embrace the new ones.
Striking a balance between the origin and the new is a challenge many are unable to meet.
Both parents are usually working full time to make ends meet while children are in English-only schools all day.
I can see, since becoming a parent, how one can go from being fluent in a native language to barely using it to communicate with his or her own children.
My hope is that having books that families can read together will set a foundation for using the language in everyday conversations.
It is one of the most important personal goals I have ever taken on.
It is daunting, for sure, but I trust the support of my community to take it from being a goal of being an accomplishment we can all celebrate.
