By identifying the language level of your ELL student, recognizing the behavioral issues that often arise from linguistic frustration, understanding some common ELL teaching myths, and—most importantly—providing a welcoming environment for your students, you can help ELLs succeed.
This diagram shows the six stage of ELL learning.The student begins with “entering” the new culture, hopefully to advance throughout the stages to finally “reaching” with their knowledge.
The English language they do speak probably consists of about 500 survival words, and they may repeat everything you say.
Provide some non-language activities for the class so as not to exhaust the ELL student
Use sensory, graphic, and interactive aids in your lessons
Ask students if they have something they want to tell/share with you.
They understand parts of the lesson and simple directions.
Strategies for teaching English language learners at the beginning stage:
Be willing to accept one or two word responses, or visual representations
Instead of asking students if they understood, ask what they understood, in order to find their true level of comprehension
Students can now communicate with simple phrases and use general vocabulary, and they ask simple questions that may not be grammatically correct.
They also can read short stories, as well as understand charts, graphs, and other questions with visuals.
Continue to accept short responses and provide graphic aids in lessons
They are willing to express their opinions and ask for clarification.
The student’s writing at this stage contains many errors as they attempt to use new grammar, which is a good sign as long as it does not impede the meaning.
Continue to provide support in content areas, especially History and Government
Strategies for teaching English language learners at the bridging stage:
Continue to provide support for complex content, but teach and assess the student as you would the proficient English speakers
There still might be occasional gaps in the student’s knowledge of American culture or lore.
The student should now be questioned like their proficient peers and expected to perform without additional language support
It is important to treat newcomers as you normally treat the rest of the class at this stage so that they don’t feel different or as if they will never be treated like the rest, no matter how hard they work or how good their English level becomes.
