The journey to resettlement is a long and difficult process.
In simple terms, a refugee is a person who left his or her country to find a new home in a safe place.
“A person who, owing to a well-founded fear of being persecuted for reasons of race, religion, nationality, membership of a social group or political opinion, is outside the country of his nationality and is unable or, owing to such fear, is unwilling to avail himself of the protection of that country.”
In some cases, the government of the country the individual has fled is directly engaging in the persecution of that individual.
After crossing the border into a neighboring country, refugees typically register with the United Nations.
Countries that have signed these conventions agree to the principle of “non-refoulement,” which means they will not force refugees to return to any home countries in which they would face danger or persecution.
Refugees must register with the United Nations Higher Commission for Refugees (UNHCR).
The average refugee spends more than ten years in a refugee camp.
All this takes its toll on refugee families—not only on parents or refugees born in camps, but on second and third generations of refugee children born to parents who grew up in camps.
However, they often face severe discrimination and abuse, especially if they do not have authorization to work in the new country.
The United States today also has a significant population of Central and South American migrants who, while not officially labeled refugees, are fleeing their homes in search of economic and physical safety.
The first solution is voluntary repatriation or returning to their home country when they no longer feel at risk of persecution.
The second solution offered by the United Nations is local integration, or settling permanently in the country to which the refugee initially fled.
Today’s current crises on European borders exemplify the challenges both refugees and receiving countries face during forced migration—refugees are not allowed (or not able) to settle safely in the second countries to which they flee, nor are they allowed to pass through these countries to reach another, safer destination.
Globally, only about 1% of refugees are ever resettled to a third country.
In addition to helping the most vulnerable population, resettlement is a way that wealthier nations can show support to the (typically) developing nations hosting large refugee populations.
After registering as refugees with the United Nations, refugees and their families can apply to be resettled.
Before admission to the United States, each refugee must undergo an extensive interview, screening, and security clearance process.
Currently, Congress helps fund nine voluntary agencies (the International Rescue Committee, US Conference of Catholic Bishops, and others) through the Department of State’s Reception and Placement Program.
After the voluntary agency has determined a placement, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) helps arrange travel to the United States.
After waiting for many years, this can happen quite quickly, often in a matter of weeks.
The demographics of these groups have shifted since the beginning of our resettlement program in the 1970s, which first began as a relatively homogeneous program to resettle Southeast Asian refugees after the Vietnam War.
Today, the largest groups come from Iraq, Burma, Somalia, Bhutan and the Democratic Republic of the Congo, as well as unaccompanied minors from Central and South America.
When refugees arrive in the US, the voluntary agency provides basic resources to meet the refugees’ immediate needs for the first 30 days, such as housing, food, and medical care.
After the first 30 days, refugees become eligible for eight months of federal benefits (cash and/or medical) through the Office of Refugee Resettlement and contracted state offices.
Refugees are expected to become self-sufficient after eight months.
This Somali uses film to teach refugee youth the importance of education.
The Refugee Center Online envisions a world of peace and justice in which refugee resettlement is unnecessary.
RCO respects and celebrates refugees’ wealth of knowledge and life experience.
The scale of the current refugee crisis, the magnitude of the trauma, and the spreading and terrible results of failed integrations around the world require innovative responses.
Historically, the United States has been known as a country that welcomes diversity and affords immigrants enormous opportunities.
Refugees and immigrants often struggle to learn English and face difficulties in finding living wage jobs, adequate housing, healthcare, education, and transportation.
As a result, newcomers largely feel marginalized or isolated and are systematically underemployed and economically disadvantaged .
Our goal is to help millions of foreign-born US residents create new lives for themselves and their families.
DACA gives young undocumented immigrants: 1) protection from deportation and 2) documents to build a life in the United States such as a work permit, a driver’s license, and a social security card.
If not, they will lose their jobs, be subject to deportation, have their families fall apart.
These are people that have been educated in the United States and now are building a life worth living.
Below is a post I wrote on my Facebook profile after Jeff Sessions, the Attorney General of the United States, gave a speech on September 5th.
My Facebook post.
I am a DREAMER and I have DACA.
I am going to share this, not to show off, but to show the country what DACA has done for me in this past 4 years.
Jeff Sessions said this: “[DACA| denied jobs to hundreds of thousands of Americans by allowing those same jobs to go to illegal aliens.”
In college, when I volunteered at a Research Facility, they told me that I did more in 6 months as a Freshman than what other Seniors have done in a year.
I didn’t steal any American’s job.
I didn’t have the luxury to get student loans or work for someone while I was going to college.
What I did instead is opened an LLC and work as a contractor.
Because I didn’t see the obstacles, I only saw opportunities.
I graduated college in less than 4 years with 2 Bachelor’s Degrees, S0 in student loans, and a job with a Fortune five hundred company at the time.
They saw potential in me that they didn’t see anywhere else.
While President Trump is signing a document to rescind DACA, this year I signed a check for over $28,000 to pay my taxes of 2016.
I too am an asset to this country.
And this is only due to the opportunity that President Barack Obama gave me in 2013.
This year, I hired my first employee and the business is growing.
This is what I have been able to achieve in four years.
Thank you for all the people that have supported me in this journey.
I am hopeful that Congress will pass a bill before March 5th and be implemented on time so that the 800,000 of DACA recipients will still be able to have normal lives in this country.
