I have been in the United States of America for the last four years, but gladly say that I have never been subjected to any form of racism or discrimination both at work and outside of work.
A friend of mine has been racially abused outside of the mall of America in the state of Minnesota at a parking ramp.
I am one guy who does not retaliate in the event of chaos and fight unless it is so tempting and life threatening such as physical attack.
I also would make sure I do not retaliate as it may lead to a fight and serious consequences.
Usually, most of the forms of racism and discrimination happen willingly outside job.
I also understand that my safety is my number one priority.
I think the best way to address any form of racism or discrimination is to tell the person doing it to stop in a polite manner and if he or she does not listen to, seek help through co-workers and eventually to law enforcement officers as the last option.
If they don’t listen, make sure to inform the supervisor who will follow it up with the management and the perpetrator.
For example, restricting or denying someone something a person is entitled to because of their race, religion or background.
No one has the right to abuse anyone or subject anyone to any form of racism.
Lastly, I would say speaking up for yourself and having the courage to share and disclose whatever you think is not right that you are experiencing is very important, so say something.
