I noticed that most of the students who identified themselves as real minorities were sitting by themselves on one side, whereas, most of the other students who identified themselves as Caucasian or by any means necessary not minorities were sitting by themselves on the other side.
I had gotten used to it like a student gets used to his schedule; after a few weeks, he or she does not need to carry it anymore.
I always wondered where this behavior came from.
The teacher welcomed the class as usual and started off reading the seating chart.
She knew who the tough dudes were, and knew how to mix all of us to form a perfect class.
The teacher asked her if there was anything between them but the girl had no big reasons other than the student was black.
Many people in the room were shocked; at least I was.
It got worse when the girl kept talking non-sense such as “I don’t want to sit with black people because I don’t like them.” As one could imagine, the minority student stood up, all jacked up ready to fight this girl.
It was close and the teacher went between them and called the Administration Office.
I have to admit, that incident did not make me angry.
After all, we must be aware that it is a Mind Virus; a negative way of thinking based on one’s experience or circumstances that has occurred in one’s life.
So, I was aware that she could have gotten such mind virus from anywhere from her past; potentially from parents, environment, grandpas, and maybe from her distant cousins.
What really caused me to be angry about the whole incident was how the authorities at school took care of the incident.
I will admit it, my counselor pulled it off smoothly.
She said something along these lines; “Wilson, I know English is difficult for you and I am afraid that the computer class I gave you might not be the right fit for you this semester.” She told me she was thinking about giving me my PE class back so that way my schedule is not too heavy for the semester, and I can take the computer class in the fall when I’m a sophomore.
I slowly nodded not because I agreed but because the whole thing seemed to be troublesome to me.
She changed my schedule right at that meeting and switched my computer class to a PE class like I had dreamed of before.
The feeling of pain that perused through my body.
I knew something was missing and it was my responsibility to find it or it would be forgotten.
The next morning, I chose to skip my class on the schedule and chose to go to the library to find out what was taking place in the computer class that I previously had before.
It created a trauma, a virus in my head that would have caused me to be angry for the rest of my life if I wanted to.
I found out that a great number of minority students were removed from the class!
In other words, he has used profanity in class and against the “teacher” that day when the incident occurred.
It is unbelievable how much we can learn when we are hungry to discover the truth.
Finding this out, I felt like my IQ increased as my dignity and all I was ever worth wore down like a soap bar melts on body when water gets involved.
As angry as I was, I was aware that a fourteen-year-old not only a minority, but a refugee who is already not active with the school system, skipping classes, and thug-like would never be heard by the authority.
To the best of my reasoning, it had to be a decision carefully reviewed by the authority at school.
Finally, I chose to keep the whole situation to myself for one reason.
I feared that if I told my friends, some would attempt to throw fists when no man wins a case against his punisher.
I knew telling my friends would do no good, but get them hyped about the depth of such inequality.
Instead, I told some friends about this incident a few months later and I explained why we had to take summer school to complete the computer class, and why we were given free city buses from our high school.
Some refugee students that I took summer school with for the computer class are still not away why their classes were changed and why their schedule shifted.
We had to take city buses that took almost two hours to get to school.
I went back to school the following year and despite all the trials I faced along the way, I kept my eyes wide open and ready to overcome any obstacle that got between me and my goals.
After all, I am a survivor.
