Citizenship: It was all worth it when they called her name | Refugee Center Online
When I received my citizenship I was in the second grade.
With years of pain and suffering, the moment we were given our citizenship certificates meant our right to life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.
As if that wasn’t enough she studied at the table along with us on the bus while dropping us off at school and then going to work.
All in a day’s time was literally the story of my mom’s life leading up to her citizenship test and interview.
It was all worth it when they called her name.
It was great to finally be part of the country we had lived in for a while.
We were all so different in our life experiences and in the path we took to get there but one thing united us was our want and our coming to belong to this country.
It is important to study all the material and know them inside and out.
Mom worked very hard to fully understand the material and it is important for others to do the same.
Citizenship Ceremony By doing all this my mother got to that special day, the citizenship ceremony.
That day was so great, it had finally affirmed and confirmed our right to be contributing members to the American society.
