Cultural awareness | Gaining cross-cultural communication skills | Refugee Center Online
Building cultural awareness into our lives
But it is possible to become more aware of our own values and assumptions and embrace the positive things that a multicultural society has to offer.
Newcomers will undoubtedly undergo some or all of the stages of adaptation.
Understanding the common stages of cultural adaptation will help you support newcomers along their path to integration and success in their new home.
Common triggers that can propel families (or move them back) within one stage or another include the end of refugee cash assistance; finding or losing a job; experiencing discrimination or racism; students achieving school milestones; or families making connections to either other Americans or other refugees.
Yet we all have our own cultural assumptions and biases—things we carry with us without even realizing we hold these values.
You could test yourself on bias in any or all of the following areas:
Were you surprised by your results?
Awareness is the key to cultural competence.
Take this as an opportunity to build your own self-awareness.
Refugees arrive with a host of past life experiences that are both difficult and confusing.
As you practice cultural humility, you will be giving your newcomer neighbors the time and a safe space to make mistakes and to work through insecurities and fears.
Cultural humility can involve recognizing that different, even conflicting, cultural perspectives can be equally legitimate
Questioning the primacy of our own perspective and values
Clarifying what is expected
Accepting the creative tension of holding two or more different perspectives
One aspect of cultural humility means being aware that most non-verbal communication is determined by cultural context, and it may be misunderstood by those who are not yet familiar with American culture.
Fortunately there is a wealth of information about diverse cultures and nations on the internet.
The Cultural Orientation Resource Center produced these Refugee Backgrounders and Culture Profiles, which include a population’s history, culture, religion, language, education, and resettlement needs, and brief demographic information.
The RCO’s Educating Refugee and Immigrant Students course was created for teachers who have refugee students in their classrooms.
You are welcome to access and use this information on teaching refugee students.
Arbert shares his story of finding himself and finding a better future.
My parents were active members of the church, always waking me up early just to go to some church event on a Saturday morning or pinching me awake anytime I fell asleep in church.
I guess I called myself a Christian.
All I had known was what it was like to be a Christian.
I was one of those who acted like a Christian only when I was at church and around people from church.
The older I became the less I started going to church.
I would text some friends and tell them to save me a seat.
We would then wait until the Pastor is praying to walk out of the church, get into our cars, and go.
I felt as if it would be a lot better if I left the place then to hear a boring sermon that is going to put me to sleep.
Deep down I knew it was wrong but I hated telling people that I was wrong.
That is why I never admitted to being wrong.
I hated feeling as if I was inferior to someone.
My temper had worsened.
I was hanging out late with my “friends,” lying more to my parents than I ever did before.
I was a wild child.
You could tell that I did not live to the example of Christ.
I knew that if I didn’t change soon enough my life would go down a downward spiral, which it had already begun.
It wasn’t until one day I was driving my mother to the hospital to see my aunt who had just given birth.
I stayed out in the hallway and patiently waited for her.
They had gone to see their daughter and their grandson.
Doctors said she would be alright, but unfortunately she was not.
At that moment the couple knew that their daughter was gone.
He got down on his knees and the first thing he did was pray.
This man was heartbroken and devastated but still praised God.
I saw how the man was heartbroken but he knew that he would one day see his daughter in heaven.
That man made me realize that there are true Christians out there.
I kept it to myself.
I stopped going to parties, I started listening to my parents, and I started appreciating those around me.
Why don’t you want to party anymore?” I just look at them and tell them, “Hey, I have changed; it’s time for me to live for the right reasons.”
I have hills I have to climb, but I always get over them.
That he is my Shepard guiding me all the way, and I keep striving on.
I do not live for my glory but for his.
