Three of the RCO’s staff members recently gave presentations at each of their children’s schools about refugees.
By The Refugee Center Online April 30th, 2017 Blog
Make sure the kids feel safe
Try to talk about refugees in an environment where the children already feel secure.
If you are giving a presentation at a school or if you are a teacher, present in students’regular homeroom classroom rather than in a gym or new environment.
Build cultural awareness into your daily life
To help kids understand, you can read books about refugees.
The Colour of Home by Mary Hoffman is a children’s book about a boy from Somalia coming to the United States that can be read to elementary school kids or just used for the pictures for preschool kids (warning: it does include a reference to guns).
For example, children might talk about how bright the houses were in the images in the book in comparison to many of the houses where they live in the United States.
When you eat food from different places, talk to your children about immigrants and how these different cuisines were brought to the US.
Besides, kids enjoy experimenting with music, rhythm and movement while exploring other cultures.
Let children know that though it is very hard to be a refugee, refugees themselves bring many assets with them to America.
Children often first become interested in learning a foreign language, for example, because of someone they know.
Research shows refugees contribute positively – help your children understand the assets refugees bring.
Give kids a chance to be active before you talk about hard subjects
For little kids, making the lesson active will help them pay attention.
She had each of the kids pick their favorite animal and then make noises and move like that animal.
Doing something physical is a good way to start talking about a subject that may be hard for you approach. Encourage questions
Be prepared for these questions and realize children often are paying close attention to the world around them.
They paid a lot of attention and had really good questions and observations,” said Marifer.
Children can accept and understand situations that don’t have easy answers.
For example, when Miranda Kaiser, RCO’s co-president, did her presentation to a group of children in Italy, they had many questions about whether or not their parents should give money to the immigrants they see on the street.
She then helped them explore what circumstances could lead people to ask for money on the street and other ways that the children and their families might give assistance.
Here is one game, adapted from the UNHCR, you can play with kids: Give each child a balloon and asked each child to share what they need to feel happy and safe.
Then, have one child stand in the middle of the circle while the other kids toss their balloons into the air and say out loud what they needed to be safe.
Older kids can play online games or participate in a simulation game.
Kids can take action, such as decorating a “Welcome Home” sign to give to a new refugee family, writing letters, or collecting items for a local refugee family.
Do you have more ideas or need more guidance?
Our friends at Bridging Refugee Youth and Children’s Services also have a great page full of more resources on teaching children about refugees.
Being able to speak knowledgeably about refugees will help you teach your children about refugees in an empathetic and strength-based manner.
Here are 10 ways we are making a difference:
Over the past 6 months, over 100,000 visitors have used the Refugee Center Online’s website to help them rebuild their lives in the United States.
Our 5 most visited pages are: 1) Understanding American Culture; 2) Scholarships for Refugees and Immigrants; 3) Rights and Laws in the United States; 4) Family Reunification (bringing one’s family to the U.S. after resettlement) and 5) Online English Language Learning resources.
One of the most common requests the RCO receives is for information on scholarships for newcomers.
“Knowing there are opportunities like this scholarship make me believe I can succeed,” said Felix.
We currently have 2,000 active learners enrolled in our free U.S. Citizenship Exam Preparation Class.
One feature of our class is that the final practice quiz consists of all 100 actual questions with 10 randomly generated questions on each quiz.
Our initial data showed that, of those who have completed the course, already over 40% have become U.S. citizens.
I was trying to study just the questions and answers before I found the Citizenship class, but it wasn’t sticking in my head.
I found that having read about the history I found it easier to answer the questions and remember the answers because I knew what I was talking about then.
3) Connecting newcomers to resources: “In Your City”
It is a searchable tool that is easy to use and helps link newcomers to services and programs in their communities, both urban and rural.
The RCO created In Your City to overcome this problem and help connect newcomers to existing sources of support.
She had initially been resettled in a large city, but could not afford to live in the city on her minimum wage job.
Fatima was nervous to move somewhere without many services for refugees.
Fatima also used the database to find a low-cost health clinic near her house where she could take her son.
4) Easing Fears after the Executive Order
Over 20,000 individuals viewed these pages and organizations working with refugees around the country printed the translations and gave them out.
Some of the most visited pages were the ones translated into languages that are very hard for newcomers to find: Karen, Nepali, and Dari, for example.
“It felt like we were being targeted by the government again, this time just for being refugees,” said Poe.
“I have a friend who had gone to Thailand to see his mother because she was sick,” said Poe.
Poe found the information he needed on the Refugee Center Online website, in Karen language, so that he could help his friend.
There was no other place to find information in Karen.
We are glad to have a place to find the information we need,” said Poe.
In partnership with Church World Service (CWS), the RCO has created numerous resources to ensure newcomers are aware of their rights and are trained to respond to threats and racism.
We then hosted two webinars to train newcomers, focusing on those groups being targeted right now through the proposed travel ban.
Omar, a refugee from Somalia, said: “This information is really important for our communities right now.
This information helped them better understand what they can do and helped them feel safe.”
In partnership with Ontario 8c School District and with support from the Oregon Community Foundation, the RCO piloted our Educating Refugee and Immigrant Studentsonline professional development course with 15 educators, administrators, and school professionals.
Fortunately, the District was committed to helping their teachers and staff gain the necessary skills to help meet the unique needs of their newcomer students.
100% of participants enrolled in the pilot program said they would both recommend the course to another educator and that they had already implemented something they learned from the course.
The course is a great tool for on-the-ground organizations who can use it to prepare volunteers and employees, as well as for any community member who simply wants to understand how to best interact with their newest neighbors.
Those who take the course self-report better understandings of the resettlement process, more confidence in their abilities to interact in cross-cultural environments, and a better idea of how to support individuals who have fled war, violence, and other trauma.
It also gave me knowledge on how to understand and gain cross-cultural communication skills.” -Lisette, one of the individuals who completed the course and a refugee family mentor in her own community.
One important value of the RCO is to respect and celebrate the wealth of experience newcomers bring with them to the United States.
This allows us to learn from one another and enriches the leadership of the organization with many different perspectives.
All of our newcomer board members bring tremendous experience and understanding of the needs of newcomers, as well as the ability to speak for and support their communities.
Mohy Omer not only serves on the RCO’s board but is also a delegate to the United States Refugee Congress.
I am incredibly grateful to be a part of this organization and to work with such a tremendous, committed team.” Mohy believes being a good leader means continually asking yourself why you are here and what the deeper reason is for dedicating one’s time to a cause.
9) Helping newcomer moms finish high school
It took us longer than we expected to build our curriculum because of the intense amount of effort required to make the curriculum accessible for low-literacy individuals and those with a wide variety of educational backgrounds including interrupted educations.
As we expected, the majority of these learners are women – over 70%.
These women often have fewer opportunities to integrate into U.S. society or finish their educations.
“I came to the United States when I was 19, with one baby.
When I found the GED program, I was so happy to study.
The proudest day for me will be when I finish school.” -A refugee mom in the course
Our Refugee Voices blog is a place where we feature stories written by newcomers, for newcomers.
It’s a place for newcomers to share their ideas about how to settle into life in the United States.
Pakshan, a refugee from Iraq, both reads and writes for Refugee Voices.
I am happy to share what I learned to help other refugees when they come to the USA.”
We could not do our work without the support of individuals like you.
Our goal is to help newcomers build successful new lives and become contributing members of strengthened communities.
