I recently had the opportunity to speak with Chanrithy Him about her new book,Rise of the Golden Aura.
Him is aPublisher, International Speaker, and Author of the Internationally Acclaimed Award-winning memoir, When Broken Glass Floats: Growing Up Under the Khmer Rouge.
Him has had a lot of experience in life.
“Refugees have amazing strengths and fortitude to tackle challenges in life and strive to work hard to make our lives better as well as others around us because of what we endured in our countries and in refugee camps,” Ms.
I have suffered.
Him is a child survivor.
She lost her parents and her siblings during the Khmer Rouge regime.
She rose to become a voice for the voiceless and a voice for International importance.
Him combines her experiences to educate, inspire and empower her readers, hosts, and audiences around the world.
With this campaign and new book, her goal is to make a positive impact on our world society.
Him says challenges never stop.
She says people are going to try to put you down.
You have to believe in yourself and trust that you are capable of doing what you set out to do.
“Some people do not know that I am intelligent and a natural storyteller.
But she has learned to forgive after her painful journey of writing her memoir When Broken Glass Floats “I persevere to love” says Ms.
She says forgiveness is the only way out to personal freedom and happiness.
Him came to the United States at sixteen years of age, she wanted to become a doctor and to tell stories.
Shortly after, Ms.
Instead, she decided to take on the other route of becoming a storyteller.
As her mission in life gets bigger with her groundbreaking new paranormal romance novel, Rise of The Golden Aura, her purpose remains the same.
It is to inspire and empower readers around the world” says Ms.
When asked about her advice for young refugees and immigrants who wish to become authors and storytellers like her, she states the following: In anything, you set out to do in life “Follow your heart and follow your passion.”
Growing up in Cambodia, Chanrithy was captivated by the romance and mystery
Dragon, she began writing Rise of the Golden Aura.
