The Embassy has very limited storage available for holding applicants’ cell phones on the day of their interview, but there is no facility at the U.S. Embassy to store any of the other above listed prohibited items. Applicants should make arrangements to store these items before entering.
When applying for a nonimmigrant visa, you are required to upload a digital photo and you must also bring one photograph to the U.S. Embassy on the day of your interview. You will also be fingerprinted when you arrive for your interview at the U.S. Embassy or Consulate.
Submitting photos that do not meet these criteria can delay the visa application process. The photos to be submitted with your Form DS-160 confirmation page must have been taken within the last six months, and must meet the guidelines shown in the image below:
Your head height, measured from the top of your head, including the hair, to the bottom of your chin, must be between 50% and 70% of the photo's total height. Your eye height, measured from the bottom of the photo to the level of your eyes, should be between 55% and 70% - or roughly 2/3 - of the photo's height.
Do not wear a hat or head covering that obscures the hair or hairline, unless worn daily for a religious purpose. Your full face must be visible, and the head covering must not cast any shadows on your face.
Visa applicants are fingerprinted by the U.S. Embassy or Consulate as part of the visa interview. During the visa application process, usually at the interview, an ink-free, digital fingerprint scan will be quickly taken.
